ily with \\(k=1\\)\n\nWe construct a family containing exactly one sunny line.\n\n1. **Lines kept unchanged.**  \n   Keep the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_1:\\;x=1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Keep the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=3,4,\\dots ,n).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sunny line.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   S:\\;y-1 = 2\\,(x-2)\n   \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y = 2x-3 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S\\) is \\(2\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore \\(S\\) is sunny.  \n   The line \\(S\\) passes through the points \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\).\n\n3. **Additional non‑sunny line.**  \n   The point \\((2,2)\\in T_{n+1}\\) is not covered by any of the lines listed so far.  \n   Introduce the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\;y=2 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   This line is non‑sunny (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).\n\n4. **Verification of the covering.**  \n\n   * If \\(a\\ge 3\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (by (8)).  \n   * If \\(a=1\\) then \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(V_1\\) (by (7)).  \n   * The points \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) lie on the sunny line \\(S\\) (by (9)).  \n   * The point \\((2,2)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\) (by (10)).  \n\n   These cases exhaust all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) because every point satisfies either \\(a\\ge3\\), \\(a=1\\), or \\((a,b)=(2,1),(2,2),(1,3)\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) belongs to at least one line of the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L^{(1)}=\\{V_1,V_3,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\cup\\{S,H\\},\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   which consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines (the vertical lines are distinct, and neither \\(S\\) nor \\(H\\) coincides with any vertical line).  \n   By construction, precisely one line of \\(\\mathcal L^{(1)}\\) (namely \\(S\\)) is sunny.  Therefore \\(k=1\\) is realizable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Trivia