t lines; \\(S\\) is the only sunny line.\n\nWe verify that every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L^{(1)}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge 3\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (by (5)).  \n*If \\(a=1\\)*, then \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(V_1\\).  \nThe remaining points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) have \\(a=2\\).  \nFor \\(a=2\\) we have \\(b\\le n-1\\).  \nThe points \\((2,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) lie on \\(S\\) and \\(H\\), respectively, because  \n\n\\[\n1=2\\cdot2-3,\\qquad 2=2 .\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((2,3)\\) (present when \\(n\\ge 4\\)) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n3=2\\cdot2-1=2\\cdot2-3+2,\n\\]\n\nhence it also lies on \\(S\\).  \nAll other points with \\(a=2\\) have \\(b\\ge 4\\) and therefore violate \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\nThus every element of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is covered, and exactly one line of the family is sunny.  \nTherefore \\(k=1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  No family can contain two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least two sunny lines.  \nSelect three points of \\(T_{n+1}\\):\n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,n),\\qquad \nP_{2}=(n,1),\\qquad \nP_{3}=\\Bigl(\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor,\\;\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\Bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll three satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\), hence belong to \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*No two of \\(P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}\\) can lie on a non‑sunny line.*  \nIndeed, a non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) or has equation \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThe three points have pairwise distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, and their sums equal \\(n+1\\neq0\\); consequently none of them lies on a line of those three types.\n\nTherefore each of the three points must be covered by a **different** line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal