h the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – The complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   Every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) has its first coordinate \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \n   All these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.\n\n2. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Keep the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the vertical lines \\(x=3,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   Replace the line \\(x=2\\) by the sunny line \\(S:\\;y=2x-3\\) (slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   The point \\((2,2)\\) is still uncovered, so we add the horizontal line \\(H:\\;y=2\\) (non‑sunny).  \n   The resulting family consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines and contains precisely one sunny line, namely \\(S\\).  \n   A short case analysis shows that every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) belongs to at least one of these lines.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Assume that a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least two sunny lines.  \n   *Every* sunny line has a slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently two distinct sunny lines intersect in a unique point.  \n   Consider the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{1}=(1,n),\\qquad P_{2}=(n,1),\\qquad P_{3}=(\\lfloor\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor,\\;\\lceil\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\rceil).\n   \\]\n\n   All three belong to \\(T_{n+1}\\).  \n   No two of them can lie on the same non‑sunny line, because the only non‑sunny directions are horizontal, vertical and the line \\(x+y=0\\); the three points have pairwise distinct \\(x\\)‑coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and their sums are all equal to \\(n+1\\neq0\\).  \n   Hence each of the three points must be covered by a different line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n\n   Since \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at most \\(n\\) lines, at most two of the three points can be covered by sunny lines.  \n   The third point would then have to lie on a non‑sunny line, which is impossible as just argued.  \n   Therefore a covering family cannot contain two sunny lines.  \n\n   The contradiction shows that any admissible family has **at most one** sunny line.\n\nCombining the constructions (which show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are indeed attainable) with the impossibility proof (which shows that \\(k\\ge 2\\) cannot occur) yields the complete answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Families with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(0)}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) and contains no sunny line.  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is realizable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Families with \\(k=1\\)\n\nDefine the following lines:\n\n* vertical line \\(V_1:\\;x=1\\);\n* vertical lines \\(V_i:\\;x=i\\) for \\(i=3,4,\\dots ,n\\);\n* sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  S:\\;y=2x-3\\qquad(\\text{slope }2\\neq0,\\infty,-1);\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n* horizontal line  \n\n  \\[\n  H:\\;y=2 .\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(1)}=\\{V_1,V_3,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\cup\\{S,H\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; \\(S\\) is the only sunny line.\n\nWe verify that every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L^{(1)}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge 3\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (by (5)).  \n*If \\(a=1\\)*, then \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(V_1\\).  \nThe remaining points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) have \\(a=2\\).  \nFor \\(a=2\\) we have \\(b\\le n-1\\).  \nThe points \\((2,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) lie on \\(S\\) and \\(H\\), respectively, because  \n\n\\[\n1=2\\cdot2-3,\\qquad 2=2 .\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((2,3)\\) (present when \\(n\\ge 4\\)) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n3=2\\cdot2-1=2\\cdot2-3+2,\n\\]\n\nhence it also lies on \\(S\\).  \nAll other points with \\(a=2\\) have \\(b\\ge 4\\) and therefore violate \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\nThus every element of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is covered, and exactly one line of the family is sunny.  \nTherefore \\(k=1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  No family can contain two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least two sunny lines.  \nSelect three points of \\(T_{n+1}\\):\n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,n),\\qquad \nP_{2}=(n,1),\\qquad \nP_{3}=\\Bigl(\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor,\\;\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\Bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll three satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\), hence belong to \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*No two of \\(P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}\\) can lie on a non‑sunny line.*  \nIndeed, a non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) or has equation \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThe three points have pairwise distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, and their sums equal \\(n+1\\neq0\\); consequently none of them lies on a line of those three types.\n\nTherefore each of the three points must be covered by a **different** line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at most \\(n\\) lines, at most two of the three points can be covered by sunny lines (there are at most two sunny lines by hypothesis).  \nThe third point would then have to lie on a non‑sunny line, which we have just shown to be impossible.  \n\nThis contradiction proves that a covering family cannot contain two sunny lines.  \nHence any admissible family has **at most one** sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\le 1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited families with \\(k=0\\) and with \\(k=1\\), and we have proved that \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible.  \nConsequently, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
