 the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nis admissible when \\(n\\) is odd, whereas\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nis admissible when \\(n\\) is even.  \nIn other words, a configuration with **all** \\(n\\) lines sunny exists exactly for odd \\(n\\); for even \\(n\\) at least one line must be non‑sunny.\n\n**b. Method sketch**  \n\nThe problem is reduced to a covering problem for the triangular lattice  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n*Every* non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V:\\;x=c,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H:\\;y=c,\\qquad \n\\mathcal D:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nConsequently a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on exactly one line of each family.\nIf we choose a set  \n\n\\[\nX\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;Y\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;S\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nand take the non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\{x=c\\mid c\\in X\\}\\cup\\{y=c\\mid c\\in Y\\}\\cup\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in S\\},\n\\]\n\nthen a point \\((a,b)\\) is covered iff  \n\n\\[\na\\in X\\;\\lor\\;b\\in Y\\;\\lor\\;a+b\\in S .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff** we can find\nsubsets \\(X,Y,S\\) satisfying (1) with  \n\n\\[\n|X|+|Y|+|S|=n-k .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe construction proceeds by induction on \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Step 1 – a reduction.**  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\]\n\nThese lines satisfy (1) for all points with \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \nThe points that remain uncovered are exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-a\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. a copy of the original triangular set of size \\(k\\).  \nHence the original problem is reduced to the following sub‑problem:\n\n> **Sub‑problem.**  For a given integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) find a covering of \\(T_m\\) by\n> exactly \\(m\\) *sunny* lines.\n\nIf the sub‑problem can be solved for every \\(m\\le n\\), then (2) is satisfied for\nevery \\(k\\) and we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Step 2 – solving the sub‑problem.**  \n\n*Lemma 1 (odd size).*  \nIf \\(m\\) is odd, the \\(m\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y = x-(i-1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,m)\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ncover all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\) we have \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\).\nChoose \\(i=a-b+1\\); then \\(L_i\\) has equation \\(y=x-(i-1)=x-(a-b)=b\\), so\n\\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).  The slope of each \\(L_i\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every \\(L_i\\) is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n*Lemma 2 (even size).*  \nIf \\(m\\) is even, the set \\(T_m\\) **cannot** be covered by \\(m\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the vertical column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le m\\}\\subset T_m .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is never vertical, hence it meets \\(C\\) in at most one point.\nConsequently \\(m\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(m\\) points of \\(C\\); to\ncover the whole column we would need at least \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines, each\ncontributing at most one further point of \\(T_m\\).  The total number of points\nin \\(T_m\\) equals \\(\\frac{m(m+1)}2\\).  For even \\(m\\),\n\n\\[\nm\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   = m\\cdot\\frac{m}{2}= \\frac{m^{2}}2\n   <\\frac{m(m+1)}2 ,\n\\]\n\nso even if every sunny line contained the maximal possible number\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) of points, the \\(m\\) lines would be\ninsufficient.  Hence a covering with \\(m\\) sunny lines does not exist. ∎\n\n\n\n*Lemma 3 (even size – one non‑sunny line suffices).*  \nFor even \\(m\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by \\(m-1\\) sunny lines together\nwith one vertical line \\(x=1\\) (which is non‑sunny).\n\n*Proof.*  Remove the column \\(C\\) as in Lemma 2; the remaining points form the\ntriangular set \\(T_{m-1}\\) (shifted one unit to the right).  Since \\(m-1\\) is\nodd, Lemma 1 provides a covering of this smaller triangle by the\n\\(m-1\\) sunny lines (3) with indices \\(i=2,\\dots ,m\\).  Adding the vertical\nline \\(x=1\\) covers the removed column, giving a covering of the whole\n\\(T_m\\) with exactly \\(m\\) lines, of which precisely one is non‑sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Step 3 – assembling the final configurations.**\n\n*Odd \\(n\\).*  \nFor any \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we proceed as follows.\n\n*   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non‑sunny).\n*   The uncovered points form a copy of \\(T_k\\).\n*   Because \\(k\\) is either odd or even, Lemma 1 (if \\(k\\) odd) or Lemma 3\n    (if \\(k\\) even) supplies a covering of this copy by exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n    lines.\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every\n\\(k\\le n\\).\n\n*Even \\(n\\).*  \nThe same construction works for all \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nIf we tried \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny), the uncovered set would be \\(T_n\\) itself.\nLemma 2 shows that \\(T_n\\) cannot be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines when \\(n\\) is\neven; consequently a configuration with \\(k=n\\) does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \n\n* For odd \\(n\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n* For even \\(n\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable,\n  while \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Reduction to a triangular covering\n\nA non‑sunny line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c\\;(c\\in[1,n]),\\qquad y=c\\;(c\\in[1,n]),\\qquad x+y=c\\;(c\\in[2,n+1]).\n\\]\n\nFix subsets  \n\n\\[\nX\\subseteq[1,n],\\;Y\\subseteq[1,n],\\;S\\subseteq[2,n+1],\n\\qquad |X|+|Y|+|S|=n-k .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{x=c\\mid c\\in X\\}\\cup\\{y=c\\mid c\\in Y\\}\\cup\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in S\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have exactly one vertical, one horizontal and one\ndiagonal line through it; therefore \\((a,b)\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) iff  \n\n\\[\na\\in X\\;\\lor\\;b\\in Y\\;\\lor\\;a+b\\in S .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff** there are\nsubsets \\(X,Y,S\\) satisfying (5) and (4).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Fixing the non‑sunny part\n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nX=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\},\\qquad Y=S=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\nThe corresponding non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with abscissa \\(a\\le n-k\\) are covered.  \nThe uncovered points are\n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-a\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a translate of \\(T_k\\).  Consequently the original problem is\nequivalent to the following:\n\n> **Problem P(k).**  Cover the triangular set \\(T_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n> lines.\n\nIf Problem P(k) can be solved for a given \\(k\\), then (6) together with the\nsolution of P(k) yields a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nHence we only have to solve P(k).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Solving P(k)\n\n#### Lemma 1 (odd \\(k\\)).\nIf \\(k\\) is odd, the \\(k\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y = x-(i-1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k) \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ncover \\(T_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) the integer \\(d:=a-b\\) satisfies \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\).\nChoosing \\(i=d+1\\) gives \\(y = x-(i-1)=x-d=b\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).\nEach line has slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore all \\(L_i\\) are sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (even \\(k\\) – impossibility with only sunny lines).\nIf \\(k\\) is even, \\(T_k\\) cannot be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k\\}\\subset T_k\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(k\\) points.  A sunny line is never vertical, so it meets \\(C\\) in at\nmost one point.  Hence \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(C\\);\nto cover the whole column we would need at least \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines,\neach contributing at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) further points\n(because a non‑vertical line can contain at most one point from each of the\n\\(k\\) columns).  Consequently the total number of points that can be covered\nby \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =k\\cdot\\frac{k}{2}= \\frac{k^{2}}2\n   <\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|T_k|,\n\\]\n\na contradiction. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 3 (even \\(k\\) – one non‑sunny line suffices).\nIf \\(k\\) is even, the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k-1\\) sunny lines and\nthe single vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nRemove the column \\(C\\) defined above.  The remaining points form the\ntriangular set \\(T_{k-1}\\) shifted one unit to the right:\n\\[\nT_{k-1}^{\\text{shift}}=\\{(a+1,b)\\mid (a,b)\\in T_{k-1}\\}.\n\\]\nSince \\(k-1\\) is odd, Lemma 1 provides a covering of this shifted triangle by\nthe \\(k-1\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i':\\;y = (x-1)-(i-1)\\qquad(i=2,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nAdding the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny) covers the removed column, and the\nunion of all these \\(k\\) lines covers the whole \\(T_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Completion of the proof\n\n*Case 1 – \\(n\\) odd.*  \nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (6); the uncovered points form a copy of\n    \\(T_k\\).\n*   If \\(k\\) is odd, cover this copy with the \\(k\\) sunny lines (7) (Lemma 1).\n*   If \\(k\\) is even, cover it with the \\(k-1\\) sunny lines (7) together with\n    the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (Lemma 3).  Since the vertical line is already\n    present among the \\(n-k\\) lines when \\(k\\le n-1\\), we simply replace one of\n    the existing vertical lines by the required sunny line; the total number of\n    sunny lines becomes exactly \\(k\\).\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n*Case 2 – \\(n\\) even.*  \nThe same construction works for all \\(k\\le n-1\\); the argument is identical\nto the odd case.\n\nIf we attempted \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny), the uncovered set would be the whole\n\\(T_n\\).  Lemma 2 shows that \\(T_n\\) cannot be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines\nwhen \\(n\\) is even, so such a configuration does not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ccl}\nn\\ \\text{odd}&:&\\displaystyle k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\[4pt]\nn\\ \\text{even}&:&\\displaystyle k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\nAll these values are attainable, and no other value of \\(k\\) can occur. ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete value